ail","scale":"1x"},{"src":"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Chinese_celadon_vase_Branly_71.1886.89.1.jpg/1280px-Chinese_celadon_vase_Branly_71.1886.89.1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","scale":"2x"}]},{"title":"File:Abbasid_Caravanseray_of_Nishapur_(Ribati-i-Abbasi_of_Nishapur)_-_Morning_244.jpg","leadImage":false,"section_id":17,"type":"image","caption":{"html":"One of many remaining <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Safavid_Iran\" title=\"Safavid Iran\" id=\"mwBc4\">Safavid Empire</a> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Caravanserai\" title=\"Caravanserai\" id=\"mwBc8\">Caravanserais</a> in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Iran\" title=\"Iran\" id=\"mwBdA\">Iran</a>. <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Shah_Abbasi_Caravansarai,_Nishapur\" title=\"Shah Abbasi Caravansarai, Nishapur\" id=\"mwBdE\">This particular caravanserai</a> is located in the city of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Nishapur\" title=\"Nishapur\" id=\"mwBdI\">Nishapur</a> which was one of the central Silk Road cities<sup about=\"#mwt451\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-133\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-133&quot;}}\"><a href=\"./Silk_Road#cite_note-133\" id=\"mwBdM\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwBdQ\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwBdU\">[</span>132<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwBdY\">]</span></span></a></sup> of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Greater_Khorasan\" title=\"Greater Khorasan\" class=\"mw-redirect\" id=\"mwBdc\">Greater Khorasan</a>.","text":"One of many remaining Safavid Empire Caravanserais in Iran. This particular caravanserai is located in the city of Nishapur which was one of the central Silk Road cities[132] of Greater Khorasan."},"showInGallery":true,"srcset":[{"src":"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Abbasid_Caravanseray_of_Nishapur_%28Ribati-i-Abbasi_of_Nishapur%29_-_Morning_244.jpg/500px-Abbasid_Caravanseray_of_Nishapur_%28Ribati-i-Abbasi_of_Nishapur%29_-_Morning_244.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","scale":"1x"},{"src":"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Abbasid_Caravanseray_of_Nishapur_%28Ribati-i-Abbasi_of_Nishapur%29_-_Morning_244.jpg/1280px-Abbasid_Caravanseray_of_Nishapur_%28Ribati-i-Abbasi_of_Nishapur%29_-_Morning_244.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","scale":"2x"}]},{"title":"File:Nestorian-Stele-Budge-plate-X.jpg","leadImage":false,"section_id":18,"type":"image","caption":{"html":"The <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Nestorian_Stele\" title=\"Nestorian Stele\" class=\"mw-redirect\" id=\"mwBeM\">Nestorian Stele</a>, created in 781, describes the introduction of Nestorian Christianity to China","text":"The Nestorian Stele, created in 781, describes the introduction of Nestorian Christianity to China"},"showInGallery":true,"srcset":[{"src":"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Nestorian-Stele-Budge-plate-X.jpg/250px-Nestorian-Stele-Budge-plate-X.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","scale":"1x"},{"src":"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d7/Nestorian-Stele-Budge-plate-X.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","scale":"2x"}]},{"title":"File:Buddhist_Expansion.svg","leadImage":false,"section_id":20,"type":"image","caption":{"html":"The <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Silk_Road_transmission_of_Buddhism\" title=\"Silk Road transmission of Buddhism\" id=\"mwBhU\">Silk Road transmission of Buddhism</a>: <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Mahayana_Buddhism\" title=\"Mahayana Buddhism\" class=\"mw-redirect\" id=\"mwBhY\">Mahayana Buddhism</a> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Chinese_Buddhism#History\" title=\"Chinese Buddhism\" id=\"mwBhc\">first entered</a> the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Chinese_Empire\" title=\"Chinese Empire\" id=\"mwBhg\">Chinese Empire</a> (<a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Han_dynasty#Religion,_cosmology,_and_metaphysics\" title=\"Han dynasty\" id=\"mwBhk\">Han dynasty</a>) during the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Kushan_Empire\" title=\"Kushan Empire\" id=\"mwBho\">Kushan era</a>. The overland and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Maritime_Southeast_Asia\" title=\"Maritime Southeast Asia\" id=\"mwBhs\">maritime</a> \"Silk Roads\" were interlinked and complementary, forming what scholars have called the \"great circle of Buddhism.\"<sup about=\"#mwt480\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-142\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-142&quot;}}\"><a href=\"./Silk_Road#cite_note-142\" id=\"mwBhw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwBh0\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwBh4\">[</span>141<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwBh8\">]</span></span></a></sup>","text":"The Silk Road transmission of Buddhism: Mahayana Buddhism first entered the Chinese Empire (Han dynasty) during the Kushan era. The overland and maritime \"Silk Roads\" were interlinked and complementary, forming what scholars have called the \"great circle of Buddhism.\"[141]"},"showInGallery":true,"srcset":[{"src":"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Buddhist_Expansion.svg/500px-Buddhist_Expansion.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","scale":"1x"}]},{"title":"File:Buddha_of_Miran.png","leadImage":false,"section_id":20,"type":"image","gallery_id":"mwBng","showInGallery":true,"srcset":[{"src":"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Buddha_of_Miran.png/250px-Buddha_of_Miran.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","scale":"1x"},{"src":"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Buddha_of_Miran.png/500px-Buddh